------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase T: does the instruction cache fuse adjacent .ttinsn words?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Every group below carries the same 16 `addi`s. `fuse4` and `spread4`
  additionally carry the same FOUR `.ttinsn` words and differ only in whether
  any two of them are adjacent. BlackholeA0/.../PushTensixInstruction.md says
  up to four adjacent ones fuse into a single cycle, so:
    spread4 - fuse4  ==  3 cycles  ->  the cache fuses, as documented
    spread4 - fuse4  ==  0 cycles  ->  it does not, and `riscv.ttinsn_fusion`
                                       describes something a kernel cannot reach

  variant   probe   thr     cyc/group    vs tt_pad
  t1        tt_pad  1          22.535
  t1        tt_fuse2 1          25.287        2.753
  t1        tt_fuse4 1          28.158        5.624
  t1        tt_spread4 1          28.347        5.813
    -> spread4 - fuse4 = +0.189 cycles/group   (documented: +3.000)
  t2        tt_pad  0          15.979
  t2        tt_fuse2 0          17.975        1.996
  t2        tt_fuse4 0          19.971        3.992
  t2        tt_spread4 0          19.971        3.992
    -> spread4 - fuse4 = -0.000 cycles/group   (documented: +3.000)
  t2        tt_pad  1          22.544
  t2        tt_fuse2 1          25.280        2.736
  t2        tt_fuse4 1          28.096        5.553
  t2        tt_spread4 1          28.343        5.799
    -> spread4 - fuse4 = +0.247 cycles/group   (documented: +3.000)
  t3        tt_pad  0          15.980
  t3        tt_fuse2 0          17.976        1.996
  t3        tt_fuse4 0          19.972        3.992
  t3        tt_spread4 0          19.977        3.996
    -> spread4 - fuse4 = +0.005 cycles/group   (documented: +3.000)
  t3        tt_pad  1          22.357
  t3        tt_fuse2 1          25.144        2.787
  t3        tt_fuse4 1          27.936        5.579
  t3        tt_spread4 1          28.069        5.712
    -> spread4 - fuse4 = +0.133 cycles/group   (documented: +3.000)
  t3        tt_pad  2          15.978
  t3        tt_fuse2 2          17.976        1.998
  t3        tt_fuse4 2          19.971        3.993
  t3        tt_spread4 2          19.977        3.999
    -> spread4 - fuse4 = +0.006 cycles/group   (documented: +3.000)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase C: is there a branch predictor, and what does a mispredict cost?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  `c_nt` and `c_t` execute the IDENTICAL dynamic instruction sequence -- a
  branch whose target is the address the not-taken path falls through to --
  and differ in exactly one bit: whether it was taken. The `c_xor_*` trio adds
  an `xori` so that the ALTERNATING pattern has a value to alternate on, and
  carries it in all three so the mix is matched.

  variant   thr          nt      taken        alt     alt - nt
  t1        1         1.027      3.026          -        1.999    (bare)
  t1        1         2.377      4.294      3.312        0.935    (with xori)
  t2        0         1.029      3.024          -        1.996    (bare)
  t2        0         2.027      4.020      3.020        0.993    (with xori)
  t2        1         1.026      3.027          -        2.000    (bare)
  t2        1         2.296      4.291      3.312        1.017    (with xori)
  t3        0         1.029      3.024          -        1.995    (bare)
  t3        0         2.028      4.021      3.020        0.993    (with xori)
  t3        1         1.027      3.027          -        2.000    (bare)
  t3        1         2.374      4.291      3.310        0.936    (with xori)
  t3        2         1.029      3.025          -        1.996    (bare)
  t3        2         2.029      4.020      3.015        0.986    (with xori)

  All three equal -> no predictor, or no penalty. taken > nt -> a static
  not-taken prediction. alt > taken ~ nt -> a real predictor, defeated by
  alternation. tt_sim/perf/unit_costs.yaml records the bubble as 2 cycles on
  Wormhole and 4 on Blackhole (`integer_unit.branch_mispredict_bubble`), which
  is the SIZE of a mispredict; how OFTEN one happens is what this measures.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase Q: how far does the core run ahead of the Tensix backend?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  EXPLORATORY. Nothing in the ISA documentation or either vendor tree gives
  a Tensix instruction queue depth, so there is no prediction here to
  confirm or refute -- only a number nothing has measured.

  CYCLES BELOW ARE RAW and every derived number is a DIFFERENCE of two of
  them, over the widest span available. `q_ctrl` is NOT subtracted point by
  point: the cascade evaluates all seven of its `if (p >= k)` tests at EVERY
  burst length, so its cost is constant in n rather than growing with it, and
  what does vary -- branch directions, first touch of the deeper blocks -- is
  non-monotonic. It is the noise floor, printed as such.

  TWO BURST FORMS, because the burst had to get longer and a longer
  straight-line burst is a longer INSTRUCTION STREAM:
    cascade  n = 1..128     2^p copies emitted straight-line. At n = 1024
                            this would be 4 KiB of text, in the octave where
                            phase F found a fetch cliff -- and a fetch cost
                            that grows with n is indistinguishable from
                            back-pressure, which is what this phase reads.
                            So the cascade was NOT extended.
    loop     n = 16..1024   n/16 iterations of one 16-instruction block: 64
                            bytes of text at every n, so no difference
                            between two loop points can be instruction
                            fetch. `q_loop_addi` is the same loop with
                            `addi` bodies and measures what the form itself
                            costs, back edge included.
  The forms OVERLAP at n = 16..128 and the overlap is checked below: it is
  the evidence that changing the form did not change the quantity.

  [t1 thread 1]
    cascade, raw cycles         1      2      4      8     16     32     64    128      rate
    q_ctrl                     11      7     16     11     23     25     26     22   (noise)
    q_nop                       5      9     22     35     47     55    104    183     1.214
    q_setdmareg                 5      9     28     29     45     67    101    180     1.205
    q_adddmareg                 5      9     22     36     46     62    112    306     2.321
    q_adddmareg_sync           19     18     28     37     61    109    205    397     3.000
    loop, raw cycles           16     32     64    128    256    512   1024      rate
    q_loop_addi                35     50     86    158    302    590   1166     1.122
    q_loop_adddmareg           35     44    117    309    693   1461   2997     2.938
    q_loop_adddmareg_sync      70    110    206    398    782   1550   3086     2.992
    noise floor: `q_ctrl` spans 7-26 cycles (19) across the burst lengths;
      that is what one raw point can be wrong by.
    form check, ADDDMAREG burst, cascade vs loop where both run:
             n    cascade       loop  loop-casc
            16         46         35        -11
            32         62         44        -18
            64        112        117         +5
           128        306        309         +3
      -> over n=16..128: cascade 2.321, loop 2.446 cycles per instruction
         (difference +0.125 -- the loop's own back edge is in this, and
         `q_loop_addi` above says how much of it that is)
    out to n=1024:  issuing core 2.938  |  drained 2.992  |  issue-limited baseline 1.122 (`q_loop_addi`)
    backlog in flight when the last .ttinsn returned (cycles of ThCon
    work, less 35 for tensix_sync()'s own cost at n=16):
      n=16 +0  n=32 +31  n=64 +54  n=128 +54  n=256 +54  n=512 +54  n=1024 +54  
    marginal cost between adjacent burst lengths:  16->32 0.56  32->64 2.28  64->128 3.00  128->256 3.00  256->512 3.00  512->1024 3.00  
    -> KNEE between n=64 and n=128: the marginal reaches 3.00 against a
       drained 2.992, so the core is back-pressured from there on.
    -> the backlog FLATTENED at ~54 cycles (last two doublings added
       0 then 0), clearing a 38-cycle noise floor, and at the
