Is the instrument live? The probes with a non-trivial documented cost
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  rv_mul_dep            2.001 cycles/instruction
  rv_div               33.001 cycles/instruction
  rv_load_chase         7.991 cycles/instruction
  rv_store_spread       5.167 cycles/instruction

  4 of 4 read above 1.0, so the timer resolves a real per-instruction
  cost and a 1.000 elsewhere in this run is a finding rather than a floor.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Did the new probes run at all? (a null is not an absence)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  phase G probes:        g_1024 ran  g_1152 not in this --gset  g_1280 ran  g_1408 not in this --gset  g_1536 not in this --gset  g_1792 not in this --gset  
    Exactly one intermediate is compiled per --gset, so four of the five
    read `not in this --gset` in every healthy run and that is not an
    absence. Against tt-sim all of them must read the SAME as each other:
    it models no instruction cache at all, so a flat row is forced there
    and says nothing about any hardware. `ran` with a flat row is that
    null; a probe that never executed would print nothing at all above.
  phase S probes:        s_loop_addi ran  s_co_plain ran  s_co_repeat ran  s_co_sync ran  s_solo_plain ran  s_solo_sync ran  
    The structural check for this phase is that `s_co_sync` exceeds
    `s_co_plain` at every burst length -- a drain cannot be free. If the
    two are equal the phase measured nothing whatever the verdict said.
    Against tt-sim the backlog grows without bound (its Tensix queue is a
    list append), so every slot refuses a depth and there is no verdict:
    forced, and a fact about the simulator rather than about any card.

